This curved white apartment block in Son Caliu/Palmanova is a typical product of Mallorca’s 1960s-1980s tourist boom rather than a signed masterpiece. Public records do not name a specific architect or construction firm. It sits in the municipality of Calvià, on land that shifted from a 1930s garden-city plan to dense coastal urbanization after Palma airport opened in 1960. The result was the rapid “balearización” that filled the shoreline with hotels and apartment blocks.

The building uses a reinforced-concrete frame finished in white render, with a stone-clad commercial ground floor and continuous wrap-around balconies. The semicircular plan and end-wall circular windows (some pivoting) were practical choices: they catch more light and sea breeze while giving every unit a sliver of view toward Son Caliu beach, Palmanova bay or the nearby Portals Nous marina. 

Green-and-white striped canvas awnings and green balcony screens follow a nationwide pattern. Between the 1960s and 1980s Spanish manufacturers offered only a handful of colours; green became the default because communities required uniformity and residents associated it with coolness.

From the upper floors the outlook is classic southwest Mallorca: sandy coves, pine-covered headlands and the luxury yachts of Puerto Portals a short walk away. Street level opens onto a small tiled plaza. Nearby you can swim at Son Caliu or Palmanova beaches, eat at Mesón Son Caliu, stroll the promenade, or reach Palma in 15 minutes.

The round windows remain the building’s quiet quirk: most neighbouring blocks of the same era stuck to rectangular openings.

The structure is not listed as protected heritage. It belongs to the everyday architecture that now defines the look of mature Spanish coastal resorts.

